---
import FaqLanding from '../../components/FaqLanding.astro';

const faqs = [
	{
		q: 'A hotel just keeps guest records. Does the Data Privacy Act really apply to us?',
		a: '<strong>Yes, from the first guest you check in.</strong> Names, IDs, contact details, nationality, payment cards and stay history are all personal data under RA 10173, and a passport or government ID makes part of it sensitive personal information. Any hotel or resort that records guests is a Personal Information Controller with the full set of obligations.',
	},
	{
		q: 'What about our CCTV cameras?',
		a: '<strong>CCTV is governed by a binding NPC circular, not just guidance.</strong> NPC Circular 2024-02 requires a lawful basis, notices at entrances, restricted access to footage, a written CCTV policy, and a privacy impact assessment for every system. There is no fixed retention period: keep footage only as long as your stated purpose needs, and write that period down. Cameras with facial recognition or other analytics count as profiling, which makes NPC registration mandatory at any size. Body-worn cameras on security staff fall under a separate circular (2025-01) with a 30-day retention rule. If your barangay or city has a CCTV ordinance, its minimum retention period counts as law, so you keep footage at least that long (NPC Advisory Opinion 2026-009). The same opinion, issued for a hotel, holds that audio stays switched off by default even on audio-capable cameras: recording sound anywhere is a separate, higher-risk activity that needs its own lawful basis, its own impact assessment and a visible audio notice, and guest rooms get no cameras at all.',
	},
	{
		q: 'We take bookings through Agoda, Booking.com and other OTAs. Who is responsible for that data?',
		a: '<strong>You are, once the data reaches your systems.</strong> The platform has its own duties, but your property is accountable for what it receives. Online travel agencies, your PMS and your booking engine form a data flow that needs data-sharing terms and vendor due diligence. Mapping and documenting that flow is a core part of the DPO’s work.',
	},
	{
		q: 'How long can we keep guest information?',
		a: '<strong>Only as long as you have a lawful purpose for it.</strong> Legal records, billing, and marketing with consent are purposes; habit is not. Keeping ID scans and full guest histories forever is a retention violation. We help you set a retention and disposal schedule you can defend.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Does a hotel need a Data Protection Officer?',
		a: '<strong>Yes, every hotel does, whatever its size.</strong> The duty applies to any organization that processes personal data, with no room-count or headcount threshold (IRR Section 26(a); NPC Advisory 2017-01). The DPO is your registered contact with the NPC and the person who handles guest privacy complaints.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Do we have to register with the NPC?',
		a: '<strong>Most hotels that copy guest IDs do.</strong> Registration is triggered by any one of four grounds (NPC Circular 2022-04, Section 5), and two of them catch hotels quickly: sensitive personal information of 1,000 or more individuals, which a property scanning guest IDs can reach within a single season, and 250 or more employees. CCTV analytics such as facial recognition trigger it at any size. Below the thresholds you file a notarized sworn declaration instead. The certificate is renewed yearly, only in the 30 days before it expires.',
	},
	{
		q: 'What if guest data leaks, such as a lost laptop or a hacked booking system?',
		a: '<strong>You have 72 hours.</strong> If guest data that could enable fraud, such as passport copies or card details, may have been taken, you must notify the NPC and the affected guests within 72 hours of learning of it, with a full report within five days (NPC Circular 16-03). A missed report is itself a fined infraction, so a prepared breach-response plan matters.',
	},
	{
		q: 'We are one property in a hotel group. Who registers with the NPC, us or head office?',
		a: '<strong>Head office can, and usually should, if it controls your systems.</strong> NPC Advisory Opinion 2026-004 (May 2026) confirms that a property running on group-set policies and systems is not a separate controller, so one central registration is proper, as long as it lists the systems actually used at your property: the property management system, guest ID copies, CCTV, booking channels. A property that decides its own purposes for some processing registers that part separately. Breach notices and the yearly incident report can be filed centrally too, if the protocol names who validates, who decides and who files. An independently owned property registers itself.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Is there anything we must file with the NPC every year, even with no breach?',
		a: '<strong>Yes: the Annual Security Incident Report (ASIR).</strong> NPC Circular 16-03 Section 22 requires every organization that processes personal data to log all security incidents and submit a yearly summary through the DBNMS, even a zero-incident one, whether or not it is registered. The window usually runs 1 January to 31 March for the prior year. Under NPC Advisory 2026-03 the 2025 report can still be filed until 10 November 2026; after that, administrative fines apply. We put it on your compliance calendar and file it for you.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Can you set this up for our property?',
		a: '<strong>Yes.</strong> As a TÜV Certified DPO (Cert #7000532) in Region 8, I can serve as your external DPO, register you with the NPC, build your Privacy Management Program, write your CCTV and guest-data notices, and train your front-desk team.',
	},
];
---

<FaqLanding
	title="Data Privacy Compliance for Hotels & Resorts | TÜV Certified DPO Region 8"
	description="Guest data, CCTV, OTA bookings, retention, and DPO requirements for hotels and resorts in Leyte, Samar & Biliran under the Data Privacy Act."
	badge="For Hotels & Resorts"
	heading="Data privacy compliance for hotels & resorts"
	intro={[
		"You scanned this from our Hotel Risk Summary. Hotels handle more personal data than they realise: guest IDs, CCTV, payment cards, and OTA bookings. Here is what the Data Privacy Act expects of a property in Region 8.",
	]}
	faqs={faqs}
	ctaHeading="Keep guest data — and your reputation — protected"
/>
